#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:375
msgid ""
"`GuestMode::execute` wraps the hardware virtualization transition and the "
"sensitive CPU-state updates around it. After guest state is loaded and "
"before host state is restored, OSTD prevents the run from migrating away "
"from the current physical CPU or being interleaved with ordinary kernel "
"execution. The kernel receives only `GuestRunResult`, whose VM-exit case "
"carries `GuestExitInfo`; it cannot directly issue hardware virtualization "
"instructions, or corrupt host state through this API."
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:377
msgid ""
"The interrupt and timer ports are safe policy traits. A buggy kernel-side "
"implementation may inject the wrong virtual interrupt or choose a poor timer "
"deadline, but this can only affect guest behavior or latency. It must not "
"corrupt host CPU state or host memory."
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:381
msgid ""
"The type is deliberately small. It does not own the vCPU context, guest "
"memory, or policy ports; those are supplied to each `execute` call. The "
"kernel decides what interrupt or timer model to provide, but OSTD decides "
"how and when those policy decisions are reflected in hardware."
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:385
msgid ""
"`GuestMode` performs hardware virtualization operations according to the "
"contents of `GuestContext`, so its expressiveness mostly follows from the "
"expressiveness of `GuestContext` described above. It adds two ports for "
"interrupt and timer state, which are intentionally kept out of "
"`GuestContext`. Together, the context and ports let the kernel run a guest "
"while providing virtual interrupts and timer events. `GuestRunResult` also "
"gives the kernel a direct way to handle other exit conditions besides VM "
"exit."
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:387
msgid "VMX and VMCS Lifecycle on x86"
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:389
msgid ""
"Safe and efficient VMX and VMCS lifecycle management can be implemented "
"under the `GuestMode` and `GuestContext` API design. Following [Linux's "
"state-tracking approach](https://elixir.bootlin.com/linux/v7.1.4/source/arch/"
"x86/kvm/vmx/vmx.c#L586), OSTD can map these APIs to VMX operations as "
"follows."
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:391
msgid ""
"`GuestMode::new` creates an internal VMX lifecycle guard that is privately "
"owned by the returned `GuestMode`. OSTD maintains a global reference count "
"of live guards. Access to this count is protected by a lock, which "
"serializes guard construction and destruction. When the first guard is "
"created, OSTD executes `VMXON` on every CPU. When the final guard is "
"dropped, OSTD performs coordinated teardown on every CPU: it first executes "
"`VMCLEAR` for every VMCS region that is still active on that CPU, and then "
"executes `VMXOFF`."
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:393
msgid ""
"`GuestContext::new` allocates a VMCS region, which is privately owned by the "
"returned `GuestContext`. OSTD uses statically allocated per-CPU data to "
"record the current VMCS region and the set of active VMCS regions on each "
"CPU. Each `GuestContext` also tracks the CPU on which its VMCS region was "
"most recently loaded and whether its launch state is clear or launched."
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:395
msgid ""
"`GuestMode::execute` disables preemption while operating on the per-CPU VMX "
"state, thereby pinning the execution to the current CPU. The exclusive `&mut "
"GuestContext` argument serializes operations on the corresponding VMCS "
"region, so the same VMCS region cannot be migrated or entered concurrently "
"from two CPUs."
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:397
msgid ""
"Let `A` be the VMCS region owned by the supplied `GuestContext`, and let `C` "
"be the current CPU:"
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:399
msgid ""
"If `A` is already active and current on `C`, OSTD proceeds directly to VM "
"entry without executing `VMPTRLD` or `VMCLEAR`."
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:400
msgid ""
"If `A` is active on `C` but is not current, OSTD executes only `VMPTRLD(A)`. "
"It preserves the existing launch state and does not execute `VMCLEAR`."
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:401
msgid ""
"If `A` is active on another CPU, OSTD sends a synchronous IPI to that CPU to "
"execute `VMCLEAR(A)`. The old CPU then removes `A` from its active set and "
"clears its current-VMCS record if necessary. After the IPI completes, OSTD "
"executes `VMPTRLD(A)` on `C` and adds `A` to the active set of `C`."
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:402
msgid ""
"If `A` is not active on any CPU, OSTD first executes `VMCLEAR(A)` to "
"establish a well-defined clear state, then executes `VMPTRLD(A)` on `C` and "
"adds `A` to the active set of `C`."
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:404
msgid ""
"OSTD chooses `VMLAUNCH` or `VMRESUME` according to the recorded launch "
"state. After a VM exit, `GuestMode::execute` leaves the VMCS region active "
"and does not execute `VMCLEAR` before returning. Therefore, subsequent "
"executions on the same CPU can reuse the current or active VMCS region and "
"benefit from the CPU's VMCS cache."
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:406
msgid ""
"When a `GuestContext` is dropped, OSTD first ensures that its VMCS region is "
"no longer active. If necessary, it synchronously executes `VMCLEAR` on the "
"CPU where the region is active before deallocating it. Similarly, when the "
"final VMX lifecycle guard is dropped, every CPU clears all VMCS regions in "
"its local active set before executing `VMXOFF`."
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:408
msgid ""
"All VMX instructions and lifecycle state remain internal to OSTD. The kernel "
"manages guest policy through the safe `GuestMode` and `GuestContext` APIs "
"without directly accessing VMCS residency state or issuing VMX instructions."
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:410
msgid "Building a Hypervisor With the OSTD APIs"
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:412
msgid ""
"The kernel can build a complete hypervisor by composing the OSTD objects "
"above with kernel-owned policy and device state. A minimal pseudo-code flow "
"looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:416
msgid ""
"// Create the VM and install userspace-backed guest RAM into the GPA space.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:431
msgid "// Create a vCPU context and let the kernel set the guest boot state.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:436
msgid "// Kernel-owned policy ports provide virtual interrupts and timers.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:454
msgid ""
"This example shows the intended split. OSTD owns the mechanisms needed to "
"enter and leave guest mode safely. The kernel owns VM lifecycle, memory-slot "
"policy, vCPU file semantics, interrupt-chip state, device emulation, and "
"userspace handoff."
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:456
msgid "Architecture-agnostic Design"
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:458
msgid ""
"Although the examples above are based on Intel CPUs, the current API split "
"should also support AMD CPUs and the ARM and RISC-V architectures."
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:460
msgid ""
"`GuestMode` and `GuestPhysMemSpace` are intended to be architecture-agnostic "
"APIs. `GuestTimerPort` and `GuestInterruptPort` should also be architecture-"
"agnostic."
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:462
msgid ""
"`GuestContext` and `GuestExitInfo` are intentionally architecture-specific. "
"It is both reasonable and necessary to provide one `GuestContext` API and "
"one `GuestExitInfo` type per architecture and, where necessary, per hardware "
"virtualization backend, since register state, system registers, exit "
"reasons, and exit metadata are fundamentally different across x86, ARM, and "
"RISC-V."
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:464
msgid ""
"The hypervisor implementation model exposed by the OSTD APIs is reusable for "
"ARM and RISC-V. Both architectures provide hardware-assisted second-stage "
"translation. `GuestPhysMemSpace` is exactly the abstraction for that second-"
"stage page table: it provides generic mapping, unmapping, and query "
"operations, while the internal implementation can use different "
"`PageTableConfig`s for Intel x86 [EPT](https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/"
"en/developer/articles/technical/intel-sdm.html \"Vol. 3C. 30.3 EPT\"), AMD "
"x86 [nested paging](https://docs.amd.com/v/u/en-US/24593_3.44_APM_Vol2 "
"\"15.25 Nested Paging\"), ARM [stage-2 translation](https://developer.arm."
"com/documentation/102142/0100/Stage-2-translation), or [RISC-V G-stage "
"translation](https://docs.riscv.org/reference/isa/v20260120/priv/hypervisor."
"html#two-stage-translation)."
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:466
msgid ""
"`GuestMode` also maps naturally to ARM and RISC-V. All three architectures "
"need an OSTD-controlled path that saves host state, loads guest vCPU state, "
"installs the guest physical memory space, enters the guest execution "
"environment, and returns to the kernel on a guest exit."
msgstr ""

#: src/rfcs/0003-hypervisor-support.md:468
msgid ""
"The two port traits express policy rather than hardware details. "
"`GuestInterruptPort` provides guest interrupt injection policy to "
"`GuestMode::execute`. On x86, the port may be backed by virtual [APIC]"
"(https://wiki.osdev.org/APIC) state; on ARM, by virtual [GIC](https://"
"developer.arm.com/documentation/198123/0302/What-is-a-Generic-Interrupt-"
"Controller-) state; and on RISC-V, by virtual interrupt-controller state "
"such as a virtual [PLIC](https://wiki.osdev.org/PLIC) or virtual [AIA "
"interrupt-controller](https://docs.riscv.org/reference/aia/v1.0/intro.html) "
"state. `GuestTimerPort` provides guest timer interrupt policy to `GuestMode::"
"execute`. On x86, the port may be backed by virtual [APIC timer or TSC-"
"deadline timer](https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/articles/"
"technical/intel-sdm.html \"Vol. 3A. 12.5.4 APIC Timer\") state; on ARM, by "
"[generic timer](https://developer.arm.com/documentation/ddi0487/mb/-Part-D-"
"The-AArch64-System-Level-Architecture/-Chapter-D12-The-Generic-Timer-in-"
"AArch64-state/-D12-1-About-the-Generic-Timer/-D12-1-1-The-full-set-of-"
"Generic-Timer-components?lang=en); and on RISC-V, by virtual timer state "
"exposed through an [SBI timer interface](https://docs.riscv.org/reference/"
"sbi/v3.0/ext-time.html) or the [Sstc](https://docs.riscv.org/reference/isa/"
"v20260120/priv/sstc.html) virtual-supervisor timer facility."
msgstr ""

